---
Each case pins down one way the rule could be wrong. `bynk test --mutate`
checks that claim: it changes `charge` and `total` one small way at a time,
and every change must fail at least one case here.
---
suite shop.delivery

// Kills `||` → `&&`, and the `if` condition replaced by `false`.
case "a member ships free on any basket" {
	expect charge(100, true) == 0
}

// Kills `>=` → `>`. Without a case at exactly the threshold, that mutant
// survives: a basket of £10 and one of £60 can't tell the two apart.
case "a basket of exactly £50 ships free" {
	expect charge(5000, false) == 0
}

// Kills the `if` condition replaced by `true`.
case "a penny under £50 pays standard delivery" {
	expect charge(4999, false) == 399
}

// Kills `+` → `-`.
case "the total adds the delivery charge" {
	expect total(1000, false) == 1399
}
